 "introduction_json": {
  "content": [
   {
    "content": [
     {
      "text": "A dozuki is a saw used to make fine cuts into softer woods. Originating in Japan, it allows the user to create a narrow, precise cut due to its thin blade construction.",
      "type": "text"
     }
    ],
    "type": "paragraph"
   },
   {
    "content": [
     {
      "text": "It is also unique in its design as it only cuts on the pull stroke, as opposed to the European standard (where the saw cuts on the push stroke). Accordingly, the woodworker can use gravity to their advantage when cutting with the saw, but they cannot put their weight into the cut like one could with the European saws.",
      "type": "text"
     }
    ],
    "type": "paragraph"
   },
   {
    "content": [
     {
      "text": "This guide will show you how to properly make a notch into a wood workpiece using a dozuki. All you need is the saw itself and a piece of soft wood for making test cuts.",
      "type": "text"
     }
    ],
    "type": "paragraph"
   },
   {
    "content": [
     {
      "text": "You might also be interested in Mr. WoodMan's ",
      "type": "text"
     },
     {
      "marks": [
       {
        "attrs": {
         "href": "http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Qa5wIpfhGcY",
         "target": null
        },
        "type": "link"
       }
      ],
      "text": "wonderful video",
      "type": "text"
     },
     {
      "text": " on how to use a variety of Japanese saws, including a dozuki.",
      "type": "text"
     }
    ],
    "type": "paragraph"
   }
  ],
  "type": "doc"
 },
 "introduction_raw": "A dozuki is a saw used to make fine cuts into softer woods. Originating in Japan, it allows the user to create a narrow, precise cut due to its thin blade construction.\n\nIt is also unique in its design as it only cuts on the pull stroke, as opposed to the European standard (where the saw cuts on the push stroke). Accordingly, the woodworker can use gravity to their advantage when cutting with the saw, but they cannot put their weight into the cut like one could with the European saws.\n\nThis guide will show you how to properly make a notch into a wood workpiece using a dozuki. All you need is the saw itself and a piece of soft wood for making test cuts.\n\nYou might also be interested in Mr. WoodMan's [http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Qa5wIpfhGcY|wonderful video] on how to use a variety of Japanese saws, including a dozuki.",
 "introduction_rendered": "<p>A dozuki is a saw used to make fine cuts into softer woods. Originating in Japan, it allows the user to create a narrow, precise cut due to its thin blade construction.</p>\n\n<p>It is also unique in its design as it only cuts on the pull stroke, as opposed to the European standard (where the saw cuts on the push stroke). Accordingly, the woodworker can use gravity to their advantage when cutting with the saw, but they cannot put their weight into the cut like one could with the European saws.</p>\n\n<p>This guide will show you how to properly make a notch into a wood workpiece using a dozuki. All you need is the saw itself and a piece of soft wood for making test cuts.</p>\n\n<p>You might also be interested in Mr. WoodMan's <a href=\"http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Qa5wIpfhGcY\">wonderful video</a> on how to use a variety of Japanese saws, including a dozuki.</p>",
